text:
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;
Some keep the Sabbath going to church;

I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;
I had been thirsty all the years;

The sea is a little more than a little;
And yet, as the sea is,
It is not so much as a little.

So we will leave them in the night;
And when they are gone, we will not tell;
We will be gone by sundown.

For an inch in the evening.

I had nothing to do;
I never expected it;
It's true of my fellow man,
If I could.

And, at last, the tone of the breezes;
You'll see the merchant be
Rage and insult,
Unnoticed in the hills;
And your larks will play a tune.

I could not hear the other's face;

I lost all sense of the house, --
The house that I could see
The hut they lived in;
And I could not say "No," but they were,
For they could not tell me "no."
